Subject: Supply libdispatch's use of Mach on Emscripten

Add mach_emscripten.c, built only for Emscripten against libmach's headers
(DISPATCH_EMSCRIPTEN_MACH_INCLUDE_DIR), the only file of libdispatch that
sees Mach headers, so that HAVE_MACH stays unset. It holds:

- The Mach waits of the event backend (patch 0003), forwarded to libmach's
  watches: libmach_port_watch, libmach_port_watch_arm, libmach_port_unwatch.
- voucher_mach_msg_set, _clear, _adopt and _revert, which voucher.c defines
  only with Mach vouchers, in the form libdispatch without them has: no
  voucher is attached or adopted, and libmach delivers none.
- The main queue's run-loop handle (patch 0001), as
  _dispatch_runloop_queue_handle_init makes it with HAVE_MACH: a receive
  right with a send right and, for the main queue, a queue limit of one, so
  that pokes (empty messages sent with a zero timeout) coalesce.
- The receive side of Mach channels. mach.c needs HAVE_MACH (kevent knotes
  that receive Mach messages into the event buffer, vouchers, thread special
  reply ports, workgroups and eventlinks); this file supplies the channel
  interface over a DISPATCH_SOURCE_TYPE_MACH_RECV source with mach.c's event
  order: dispatch_mach_create(_f) make an inactive channel,
  dispatch_mach_connect activates it, CONNECTED precedes every other event,
  each queued message is received with the AV trailer and delivered as
  MESSAGE_RECEIVED, a receive barrier is followed by BARRIER_COMPLETED, and
  dispatch_mach_cancel stops reception (a message received meanwhile is
  destroyed) and delivers DISCONNECTED for the receive right, then CANCELED.
  dispatch_mach_msg_create and dispatch_mach_msg_get_msg keep mach.c's
  destructors and sizes. The channel object is a serial queue whose context
  holds the channel state, a message a dispatch data object. The send side
  is not supplied: connecting with a send right or a checkin message aborts,
  and the send, reply, reconnect, no-senders and XPC interfaces stay
  undefined.

